    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most adults who lift, run or are trying to lose fat do best on roughly 1.2 to 2.2 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight per day, about 0.55 to 1.0 grams per pound. That is well above the 0.8 g/kg RDA printed in textbooks. Where you land in that range depends on your goal, your age and how hard you train. This guide gives you the number, shows the math for your weight, and explains how to hit it with food before you reach for a scoop of powder.
        </p>

        <div id="rda" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The RDA Is a Minimum, Not a Target
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Recommended Dietary Allowance for protein in the US is 0.8 grams per kilogram of body weight per day for healthy adults, set by the National Academies in its Dietary Reference Intakes. For a 70 kg (154 lb) adult that works out to about 56 grams a day. It is the figure quoted in most textbooks and behind the Daily Value on food labels, and it is routinely misread as the amount you should aim to eat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The RDA was built from nitrogen balance studies, which estimate the smallest amount of protein that stops a healthy, mostly sedentary adult from losing body protein over time. Researchers took the average requirement and added a safety margin so the figure covers nearly all healthy people. That makes it a sensible floor for preventing deficiency. It was never designed to tell you how much protein maximizes muscle growth, preserves lean mass while dieting, or supports recovery from hard training.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are also methodological reasons to think the floor itself is set low. Nitrogen balance tends to overestimate intake and underestimate losses, and newer techniques such as the indicator amino acid oxidation method have produced estimated requirements for healthy adults closer to 1.0 to 1.2 g/kg. Those studies are small and still debated, but they point in the same direction as the training research: the RDA is the bottom of the useful range, not the middle of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is simple. If you are sedentary, healthy and eat a reasonably varied diet, you probably already exceed the RDA without trying; most American adults do. If you train, are trying to lose fat, or are over about 65, the evidence supports aiming higher. The rest of this guide is about how much higher, and our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> will run the numbers for your weight and goal in a few seconds.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0.8 g/kg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">US RDA for healthy adults</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1.4-2.0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">ISSN range in g/kg for exercising people</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1.6 g/kg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Where muscle gains plateaued (Morton 2018)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">20-40 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Useful protein per meal</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="by-goal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Protein You Need by Goal
          </h2>

          <p>
            The ranges below come from the major position stands and meta-analyses rather than any single study. They are expressed per kilogram of body weight because that is how the research reports them; the next section converts them to pounds and works through real body weights.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sedentary or lightly active adults: 0.8 to 1.0 g/kg</h3>

          <p>
            If you do not exercise regularly and are not trying to change your body composition, the RDA plus a small buffer covers you. There is no strong evidence that pushing a sedentary adult to 2 g/kg builds muscle on its own, although extra protein does make meals more filling, which can be useful if appetite control is a goal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Building muscle: 1.6 to 2.2 g/kg</h3>

          <p>
            The International Society of Sports Nutrition (ISSN) position stand on protein and exercise recommends 1.4 to 2.0 g/kg per day for most exercising people. The most cited meta-analysis on the question, Morton and colleagues in the British Journal of Sports Medicine in 2018, pooled 49 trials and found that the added benefit of protein on lean mass gains plateaued around 1.6 g/kg per day, with the upper confidence limit near 2.2 g/kg. Treat 1.6 as the working floor; going to 2.2 is a reasonable way to make sure you are not one of the people for whom the average was too low.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fat loss and cutting: the higher end, about 1.8 to 2.4 g/kg</h3>

          <p>
            In a calorie deficit the body is more willing to break down muscle for fuel, and higher protein intakes help preserve lean mass while also improving fullness. A review by Helms and colleagues on lean, resistance-trained athletes in a deficit suggested 2.3 to 3.1 g/kg of fat-free mass, which for most people lands near the top of the range above. The leaner you are and the steeper the deficit, the more the higher end matters. If you carry a lot of body fat, see the section on which weight to use before you multiply.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Endurance athletes: 1.2 to 1.6 g/kg, more in heavy blocks</h3>

          <p>
            The joint position statement from the American College of Sports Medicine, the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics and Dietitians of Canada puts athletes in general at 1.2 to 2.0 g/kg. Runners, cyclists and triathletes usually sit in the lower half, rising toward 1.6 to 1.8 g/kg during high-volume phases or when they are also trying to drop weight. Carbohydrate is still the main fuel; protein is for repair and adaptation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adults over 65: at least 1.0 to 1.2 g/kg</h3>

          <p>
            The PROT-AGE study group and the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism (ESPEN) both recommend at least 1.0 to 1.2 g/kg for healthy older adults, and 1.2 to 1.5 g/kg for those with acute or chronic illness. Ageing muscle responds less to a given dose of protein, a phenomenon called anabolic resistance, so older adults who also do resistance training often benefit from the 1.2 to 1.6 g/kg range, provided kidney function is normal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pregnancy and breastfeeding: talk to your clinician</h3>

          <p>
            The US RDA rises to about 1.1 g/kg per day, or roughly 25 extra grams a day, in the second half of pregnancy and during breastfeeding. Some newer research suggests needs may be higher in late pregnancy, but this has not changed official guidance. Requirements vary with weight gain, twins and medical history, so set your target with your obstetric clinician or a registered dietitian, and check any protein powder for added herbs, stimulants or high doses of vitamins before using it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Goal</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">g/kg per day</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">g/lb per day</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Main source</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sedentary adult</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.8-1.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.36-0.45</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">US RDA (National Academies)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Building muscle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.6-2.2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.73-1.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Morton et al. 2018; ISSN</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat loss / cutting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.8-2.4</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.8-1.1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">ISSN; Helms et al.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Endurance training</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.2-1.6</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.55-0.73</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">ACSM / AND / DC joint position</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Adults 65+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.0-1.2 or more</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.45-0.55 or more</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">PROT-AGE; ESPEN</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pregnancy (second half)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about 1.1 or more</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about 0.5 or more</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">US RDA; ask your clinician</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="the-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Math: Grams per Kilogram and per Pound
          </h2>

          <p>
            To convert your weight to kilograms, divide pounds by 2.2. Then multiply by the g/kg target for your goal. A 180 lb lifter is about 82 kg; at 1.6 g/kg that is roughly 131 grams of protein a day, and at 2.2 g/kg it is about 180 grams. If you prefer to stay in pounds, multiply your weight by the per-pound figure instead: 0.36 g/lb is the RDA, 0.73 g/lb matches the 1.6 g/kg plateau, and 1.0 g/lb matches 2.2 g/kg.
          </p>

          <p>
            That last conversion is where the old gym rule of one gram per pound comes from. It is not magic, and it is not a requirement, but it happens to sit at the top of the range where the research stopped finding extra benefit for muscle gain. If one gram per pound is easy for you to hit, it is a fine target. If it is a daily struggle, 0.7 to 0.8 grams per pound captures almost all of the benefit for far less effort and cost.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Body weight</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">RDA (0.8 g/kg)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Active (1.2 g/kg)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Muscle gain (1.6 g/kg)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Upper range (2.2 g/kg)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">120 lb (54 kg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">44 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">65 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">87 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">150 lb (68 kg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">54 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">82 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">109 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">180 lb (82 kg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">65 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">98 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">131 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">180 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">210 lb (95 kg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">76 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">114 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">152 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">210 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">240 lb (109 kg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">87 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">131 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">174 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">240 g</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Figures are rounded to the nearest gram. Read across to your goal and you have a daily number. The jump from the RDA column to the muscle gain column is large: a 150 lb person goes from about 54 grams to about 109 grams, roughly double. That is the gap most people underestimate, and it is exactly the gap a protein shake is useful for closing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not chase precision beyond the nearest 10 grams. Food labels are allowed a meaningful margin of error, your weight fluctuates from day to day, and the research ranges themselves are averages across groups of people. Hitting 125 to 135 grams most days is better than hitting exactly 131 grams on a spreadsheet three days a week. If you want the arithmetic done for you, including a per-meal split, the <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> handles it.
          </p>

          <p>
            One note on the 240 lb row: if a large share of that weight is body fat, the figures in the right-hand columns are probably higher than you need. The next section explains which weight to plug in when total body weight gives a misleading answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Weight to Use If You Carry Extra Body Fat
          </h2>

          <p>
            Grams per kilogram of total body weight works well for people in a typical body fat range, because most of the studies behind the numbers recruited those people. It works less well at higher body fat, because fat tissue needs very little protein to maintain. A 300 lb (136 kg) person at 1.6 g/kg would be told to eat about 218 grams a day, a number that is hard to hit, expensive, and not supported by any evidence of extra benefit.
          </p>

          <p>
            For people with obesity, the more sensible approach is to calculate protein from a goal or target body weight. Pick a realistic weight you are working toward, not an idealized one from a chart, and multiply that by the target for your goal. If a 300 lb person sets a goal of 220 lb (100 kg), 1.2 to 1.6 g/kg of that goal weight gives 120 to 160 grams a day, which is achievable and still high enough to help protect muscle during weight loss. If you are lifting and getting leaner, you can move toward the top of that range over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clinical nutrition practice uses a similar idea. Dietitians often calculate needs from an adjusted body weight, which sits between ideal and actual weight, rather than from total weight. The exact formula matters less than the principle: base protein on the tissue you are trying to keep and build, not on fat mass you are trying to lose.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are using a GLP-1 medication such as semaglutide or tirzepatide, protein deserves more attention, not less. These drugs reduce appetite sharply, and a meaningful share of the weight lost can come from lean mass if protein intake and resistance training are neglected. Work out a target with your prescriber or a registered dietitian. When appetite is low, a shake is often one of the easier ways to get 20 to 25 grams of protein in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fat-free mass is the most precise basis of all if you know it, which is why some research reports protein per kilogram of lean mass. Most people do not have a reliable body composition measurement, though, and bathroom scale estimates can be badly off. Goal weight is usually the more practical anchor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="per-meal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Split Protein Across Your Meals
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you know your daily total, the next question is how to divide it. Muscle protein synthesis, the process that builds and repairs muscle tissue, responds to protein feedings in pulses. Feeding studies show the response rises with dose up to a point and then flattens, which is why spreading protein across the day generally works better for muscle than eating most of it at dinner.
          </p>

          <p>
            A widely cited practical target comes from a 2018 paper by Brad Schoenfeld and Alan Aragon in the Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition: about 0.4 g/kg per meal across at least four meals, which adds up to the 1.6 g/kg daily figure. For a 180 lb (82 kg) person, that is roughly 33 grams per meal. For most adults it lands between 20 and 40 grams, with the higher end for larger, older or very well trained people.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leucine is the reason the dose matters. This amino acid acts as a signal that switches on muscle protein synthesis, and research suggests roughly 2 to 3 grams of leucine in a meal produces a full response. Whey protein is about 10 to 11 percent leucine, so a 25 gram serving gets you there. Plant proteins are typically lower in leucine, which is one reason a plant serving may need to be a little larger; we cover the details in <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Body weight</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per meal (0.4 g/kg)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Daily total across 4 meals</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">120 lb (54 kg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">22 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">87 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">150 lb (68 kg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">27 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">109 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">180 lb (82 kg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">33 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">131 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">210 lb (95 kg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">38 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">152 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">240 lb (109 kg)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">44 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">174 g</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Typical eating patterns are lopsided: a light breakfast with 10 to 15 grams of protein, a moderate lunch and a big dinner with 50 grams or more. Moving 15 to 20 grams from dinner to breakfast is one of the easiest improvements you can make, and it often happens naturally if a shake replaces a low-protein breakfast. For when that shake works best around training and sleep, see our guide on <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Snacks count too. A Greek yogurt or a shake mid-afternoon turns a three-meal day into a four-feeding day without much effort, which is the simplest way to reach the four-meal pattern the research describes if you do not want to cook more often.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="absorption" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Protein Can You Absorb at Once?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The claim that your body can only absorb 30 grams of protein per meal and wastes the rest is one of the most persistent myths in nutrition. Absorption and use are different things. Your gut absorbs nearly all the protein you eat, whether a meal contains 20 grams or 80 grams; a larger dose simply takes longer to digest and enter the bloodstream.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the early studies actually measured was muscle protein synthesis over a few hours after a single dose of fast-digesting protein, usually whey, taken on its own. Under those conditions, about 20 to 25 grams maximized the response in young men, with 40 grams adding a little more after whole-body training and in older adults. That is where the 20 to 40 gram rule comes from, and it is still a useful guide for planning meals.
          </p>

          <p>
            More recent work has complicated the picture in a helpful way. A 2023 study by Trommelen and colleagues gave participants either 25 or 100 grams of milk protein after resistance exercise and tracked them for 12 hours. The 100 gram dose produced a larger and much longer-lasting anabolic response, with no sign of a hard ceiling. The extra protein was not wasted; it was used over a longer period.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein not used to build muscle is not thrown away either. It goes toward other tissues, enzymes and immune proteins, or is broken down and used for energy, just as surplus carbohydrate or fat would be. So a large dinner is not a waste, but it is still a less efficient way to stimulate muscle growth across the whole day than several moderate meals.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE MYTH</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Your body does not cap protein at 30 grams</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Nearly all the protein you eat is absorbed. Larger doses digest more slowly and keep muscle protein synthesis elevated for longer. Spread meals for efficiency, but never skip protein because a meal is already over 30 grams.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The practical rule stands: spread intake across three to five meals when you can, aim for 20 to 40 grams at each, and do not worry when one meal runs higher. If intermittent fasting or a busy schedule means you only eat twice a day, servings of 50 to 60 grams are fine, and your daily total remains the number that matters most.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="food-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Food First, Then Powder to Fill the Gap
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein powder is a convenience food, not a requirement. Whole foods bring fiber, iron, zinc and other micronutrients that a shake does not, and most people can reach 1.6 g/kg on a sensible diet built around meat, fish, eggs, dairy, legumes and soy. The smart way to use powder is to work out what your normal meals already supply and use it only for the shortfall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rough protein contents make that easy. The figures below are approximate, based on typical USDA values and common package sizes, and they vary by brand and preparation, but they are close enough to add up a normal day.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Food</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical portion</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein (approx.)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chicken breast, cooked</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 oz (113 g)</td>
