                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Here is how that plays out. A 150 lb (68 kg) person aiming for 1.6 g/kg needs about 109 grams. Eggs at breakfast (18 g), a chicken salad at lunch (35 g), Greek yogurt as a snack (20 g) and a modest dinner with fish or tofu (25 g) brings them to about 98 grams. One scoop of powder closes the gap. Without the yogurt, they would need two. That is how most people should think about powder: a tool to finish the day, not the foundation of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where powder earns its place is convenience and cost per gram of protein. A scoop of whey isolate delivers around 25 grams with little fat or carbohydrate, needs no cooking, and at typical retail prices is competitive on cost per gram with many whole-food protein sources. Plant blends serve vegans and people who do not tolerate dairy. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder</a> rankings compare options by protein per serving, cost per 25 grams of protein and third-party testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third-party testing matters more the more powder you use. A scoop a day is a modest exposure; three scoops a day for years makes contaminant levels worth checking. Look for NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice or Informed Sport on the specific product and flavor, and confirm it in the certifier's database because listings change. Our guide to <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> covers heavy metals and what the certifications actually test.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is a High-Protein Diet Safe? Kidneys and Other Caveats
          </h2>

          <p>
            For healthy adults, the evidence that protein intakes in the 1.6 to 2.2 g/kg range harm the kidneys is weak. A 2018 meta-analysis by Devries and colleagues in the Journal of Nutrition found that higher protein intakes did not meaningfully change markers of kidney function in people without kidney disease. Higher protein does raise glomerular filtration rate, the rate at which the kidneys filter blood, but that appears to be a normal adaptation rather than a sign of damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            The picture is different if you already have chronic kidney disease, reduced kidney function, or risk factors such as long-standing diabetes or high blood pressure. In those cases protein targets are usually set lower, sometimes much lower, depending on the stage and treatment. Do not raise your intake without talking to your doctor or a renal dietitian, and do not use protein powder to work around their advice. Early kidney disease often has no symptoms, so routine blood and urine tests are worth having if you are in a risk group.
          </p>

          <p>
            A few other cautions apply. People with liver disease, inherited metabolic disorders such as phenylketonuria, or a history of kidney stones should get individual advice. Anyone taking medications should check that a supplement does not contain added ingredients that interact with them. And very high-protein diets can crowd out fiber-rich plant foods, so keep an eye on the rest of your plate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drink enough water, especially if your protein intake has gone up sharply. Higher protein increases the amount of urea your kidneys clear, which modestly raises fluid needs. You do not need to force fluids, but if your urine is consistently dark, drink more.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this is medical advice. Treat these ranges as a starting point for healthy adults. If you have a medical condition, are pregnant, or are managing a weight-loss medication, set your target with a doctor or registered dietitian who knows your history.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the RDA as the goal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 0.8 g/kg RDA prevents deficiency in healthy, mostly sedentary adults. It was not designed for people who lift, run or diet. If you train or are trying to lose fat, eating at the RDA leaves results on the table and makes muscle loss more likely in a deficit. Use 1.6 g/kg as your working floor instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using total body weight with obesity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Multiplying 1.6 or 2.2 by total body weight at high body fat produces targets of 200 grams or more that are hard to reach, expensive and unsupported by evidence. Calculate from a realistic goal weight instead. You get a number you can actually hit that still protects muscle while you lose fat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backloading protein into dinner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 10 gram breakfast and a 70 gram dinner can add up to the same daily total as three 27 gram meals, but the lopsided pattern stimulates muscle less efficiently. Move some protein earlier in the day, aiming for 20 to 40 grams per meal, and the same total does more work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Living on shakes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Powder is useful for closing a gap, but three or four shakes a day can displace whole foods that supply fiber, iron, zinc and other nutrients. It also multiplies your exposure to any contaminants in the product. Build your meals first, then use one or two scoops to finish the day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring kidney health</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High-protein diets look safe for healthy kidneys, but they are not appropriate for everyone. If you have chronic kidney disease, diabetes, high blood pressure or a family history of kidney problems, get your kidney function checked and agree on a protein target with your doctor before raising your intake.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein do I need a day to build muscle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most research supports 1.6 to 2.2 grams per kilogram of body weight per day, or about 0.73 to 1.0 grams per pound. The Morton 2018 meta-analysis found gains plateaued around 1.6 g/kg, with an upper estimate near 2.2 g/kg. Pair that intake with progressive resistance training, because protein without a training stimulus does little for muscle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 1 gram of protein per pound too much?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a healthy adult who trains, no. One gram per pound equals about 2.2 g/kg, the top of the range where studies stopped finding extra benefit for muscle. It is more than most people need, though. If hitting it is a struggle, 0.7 to 0.8 grams per pound captures nearly all the benefit at lower cost and effort.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein do I need to lose weight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you are reasonably lean and lifting, aim toward the higher end, about 1.8 to 2.4 g/kg, to protect muscle in a deficit. If you have a lot of weight to lose, use 1.2 to 1.6 g/kg of a realistic goal weight instead of your current weight. Higher protein also keeps you fuller, which makes the deficit easier to sustain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you eat too much protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For healthy adults there is no clear evidence of harm at intakes up to around 2.2 g/kg, and studies in healthy people have not found kidney damage at higher amounts. The practical downsides of very high intakes are crowding out other foods, digestive discomfort and cost. People with kidney or liver disease need limits set individually by their doctor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein do older adults need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Expert groups such as PROT-AGE and ESPEN recommend at least 1.0 to 1.2 g/kg per day for healthy adults over 65, and more during illness or recovery. Because older muscle responds less to small doses, aim for 25 to 40 grams per meal and add resistance training. Check with your doctor first if you have reduced kidney function.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need protein powder to hit my target?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Most people can reach their target with meat, fish, eggs, dairy, soy and legumes. Powder is a convenient and often cost-effective way to cover a shortfall of 20 to 50 grams, which is common at higher targets. Add up what your normal meals supply first, then use a scoop or two to close the gap.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein do I need on rest days?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About the same as on training days. Muscle protein synthesis stays elevated for roughly 24 to 48 hours after resistance training, so recovery continues on rest days. Keeping intake consistent from day to day is simpler and matches what the research tested. You can move carbohydrate up or down with training load, but leave protein steady.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

